---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Code from "../../components/docs/Code.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/docs/Callout.astro";
import Params from "../../components/docs/Params.astro";
---

<DocsLayout title="Configuration" lead="Offhook is configured with environment variables, checked once at startup so a bad value fails immediately rather than on the first call. Set them on both the API and the worker. Provider keys don't go here; they live encrypted in the database as <a href='/docs/connections/'>connections</a>." slug="configuration">
        <h2 id="core">Core</h2>
        <Params title="Environment" rows={[
          { name: "ENV", type: "dev | prod", desc: "<code>dev</code> (the default) or <code>prod</code>. <code>prod</code> turns on the startup checks listed at the end of this page." },
          { name: "DATABASE_URL", type: "string", required: true, desc: "The Postgres connection string, e.g. <code>postgresql+asyncpg://voice:…@host:5432/voice</code>. It must use the <code>+asyncpg</code> driver." },
          { name: "PUBLIC_BASE_URL", type: "url", desc: "The public HTTPS address carriers call back on, for the answer webhook and the audio stream. Required in <code>prod</code>." },
          { name: "LOG_LEVEL", type: "string", desc: "Defaults to <code>DEBUG</code>. Use <code>INFO</code> or <code>WARNING</code> in production." },
          { name: "RECORDINGS_DIR", type: "string", desc: "Where call recordings are saved on local disk (default <code>recordings</code>). On hosts that wipe the disk on redeploy, mount a volume here." },
        ]} />

        <h2 id="encryption">Encryption and keys</h2>
        <p>
          Provider keys are protected with envelope encryption. Each tenant has its own data key,
          which encrypts that tenant's secrets, and a <em>master key</em> encrypts the data keys.
          The master key never goes in the database.
        </p>
        <Params title="Keys" rows={[
          { name: "MASTER_ENCRYPTION_KEY", type: "base64", desc: "The master key: base64 of exactly 32 random bytes. Required in <code>prod</code> unless you use AWS KMS. <strong>If you lose it, every stored provider key is unrecoverable.</strong>" },
          { name: "MASTER_KEY_ID", type: "string", desc: "A name for the current master key (default <code>v1</code>), recorded with each data key so older keys can still be used during rotation." },
          { name: "KEY_PROVIDER", type: "env | kms", desc: "<code>env</code> (the default) uses <code>MASTER_ENCRYPTION_KEY</code>. <code>kms</code> encrypts the data keys with AWS KMS instead." },
          { name: "KMS_KEY_ID", type: "string", desc: "The KMS key ID or ARN. Required when <code>KEY_PROVIDER=kms</code>." },
        ]} />
        <p>To generate a master key:</p>
        <Code tabs={[{ label: "Terminal", lang: "bash", code: `python -c "import os,base64; print(base64.b64encode(os.urandom(32)).decode())"` }]} />
        <Callout kind="warn">
          Keep the master key in a secrets manager, or use <code>KEY_PROVIDER=kms</code>. Never
          commit it. To rotate it, set the new key as <code>MASTER_ENCRYPTION_KEY</code>, change
          <code>MASTER_KEY_ID</code>, and keep the old key available as
          <code>MASTER_ENCRYPTION_KEY_&lt;old id&gt;</code> so existing data keys can still be
          decrypted.
        </Callout>

        <h2 id="rls">Row-level security (optional)</h2>
        <p>
          Every query is already limited to one tenant in the application code. Postgres
          row-level security adds a second layer, so a missed filter still can't return
          another tenant's data. It's off by default because it needs two database roles.
        </p>
        <Params title="RLS" rows={[
          { name: "ENABLE_RLS", type: "bool", desc: "Turns on row-level security per transaction. Apply <code>app/db/rls.sql</code> first." },
        ]} />
        <p>
          The API connects as a role that row-level security applies to. The worker and the
          carrier endpoints connect as a role that bypasses it, because they look calls up by
          job or token before they know the tenant. <code>app/db/rls.sql</code> explains the
          full setup.
        </p>

        <h2 id="dashboard">Dashboard sign-in and CORS</h2>
        <p>Only needed if you host the optional dashboard.</p>
        <Params title="Dashboard" rows={[
          { name: "CLERK_ISSUER", type: "url", desc: "The issuer of your Clerk app's session tokens, e.g. <code>https://your-app.clerk.accounts.dev</code>. The API checks tokens against its published keys." },
          { name: "CLERK_JWKS_URL", type: "url", desc: "Overrides where those keys are fetched from. By default it's derived from the issuer." },
          { name: "CORS_ORIGINS", type: "comma-separated", desc: "Browser origins allowed to call the API. Set it to your dashboard's address in production; the default only allows the local development ports." },
        ]} />

        <h2 id="dev-only">Development settings</h2>
        <p>These are for local development. In <code>prod</code>, the first two must be off or the app won't start.</p>
        <Params title="Development" rows={[
          { name: "ENABLE_PREBUILT_UI", type: "bool", desc: "Serves the browser playground, which starts calls without authentication. On by default; <strong>must be false in prod</strong>." },
          { name: "WEBHOOK_ALLOW_PRIVATE", type: "bool", desc: "Allows webhooks to private and loopback addresses, for testing a local receiver. It turns off a security check, so it <strong>must be false in prod</strong>." },
          { name: "DEV_AGENT_ID", type: "uuid", desc: "The agent the playground talks to." },
        ]} />

        <h2 id="checklist">Production checklist</h2>
        <p>With <code>ENV=prod</code>, the app refuses to start unless the master key (or KMS) and <code>PUBLIC_BASE_URL</code> are set and both development settings are off:</p>
        <Code tabs={[{ label: ".env (prod)", lang: "bash", code: `ENV=prod
DATABASE_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://voice_api:…@db:5432/voice
PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://api.your-domain.com    # required in prod
MASTER_ENCRYPTION_KEY=…                         # required in prod (or KEY_PROVIDER=kms)
ENABLE_PREBUILT_UI=false                        # must be false in prod
WEBHOOK_ALLOW_PRIVATE=false                     # must be false in prod
LOG_LEVEL=INFO
CORS_ORIGINS=https://console.your-domain.com` }]} />
        <p>Before you go live, also check that:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>The master key is in a secrets manager or KMS, not on the server and not in git.</li>
          <li>HTTPS is in front of the API, and <code>PUBLIC_BASE_URL</code> matches its address.</li>
          <li>Migrations are applied. <code>/readyz</code> reports <code>"migrations": "ok"</code>.</li>
          <li>Postgres is backed up, and you have tested a restore. The database holds every encrypted key and your whole call history.</li>
          <li><code>CORS_ORIGINS</code> lists only your real dashboard address.</li>
          <li>At least one worker is running, with time to shut down gracefully. Without a worker, calls stay <code>queued</code>.</li>
          <li>Optionally, <code>ENABLE_RLS=true</code> with the two-role setup.</li>
        </ul>

        <Callout kind="note">
          Back up the master key and the database separately, and protect both. A database
          backup is unreadable without the master key, and the master key is useless without
          the database. Keeping them apart is what makes envelope encryption safe.
        </Callout>

        <p>
          Day-to-day operations and troubleshooting are covered in <code>RUNNING.md</code> in
          the repository.
        </p>

</DocsLayout>
